# Paradox Vault — released 2026-08-15 (Pacific)

Shipped as a **forced Saturday release** under STEP 1b/STEP 4 of the nightly
routine: the game started 2026-08-11, so the one-week clock ran out tonight and
release is mandatory regardless of polish level. This is **not** a quality-based
release, and the distinction matters for anyone reading this file later.

## Critic verdicts — read this honestly

**No discipline ever recorded a passing blind side-by-side critic verdict.**
Not one. The routine's quality-based release path requires every discipline's
critic to pass a side-by-side against a named shipped AAA title; that bar was
never reached, and this release does not claim it was.

| discipline | file | critic verdict |
|---|---|---|
| textures | `textures.js` | **none recorded** — loop cut off mid-flight (2026-08-12) |
| UI / HUD | `ui.js`, `game.css` | **none recorded** — loop cut off mid-flight (2026-08-12) |
| render | `render.js` | **none recorded** — loop cut off mid-flight (2026-08-14) |
| particles | `particles.js` | **none recorded** — loop cut off mid-flight (2026-08-14) |
| audio | `audio.js` | **none recorded** — loop cut off mid-flight (2026-08-14) |

Each of the three nightly runs was killed before its discipline agents reported
back, so their work landed in the tree as `wip(...)` commits with the critic
loops still open. No independent shipping judge was run either — that is also a
quality-release requirement, not a forced-release one.

## What *was* verified before shipping, in a real browser

* `npx jest` — **5 suites / 128 tests green** (mechanics, echo-replay
  determinism, RNG stability, collision, per-vault solvability, scoring, and the
  design-invariant suite that derives each vault's true echo cost).
* `tools/smoke.js` — **13/13 checks, 0 console issues**: boots, rewind
  accounting (exactly one echo spawned and one spent, `loopIndex` +1), all ten
  vault transitions build, a late-vault rewind, and the run ends cleanly.
* Screenshot sweep at **320x568, 390x844, 844x390, 768x1024, 1440x900,
  3840x2160** — all six reach `playing`, no horizontal scrollbar anywhere, and
  the only console error is the arcade's `googletagmanager` tag being blocked by
  the sandbox proxy (present on every game page, not a game defect).
* **Touch controls driven with real pointer events** at 390x844: the floating
  virtual stick engages on the left 62% of the screen and moved the player 8.2
  tiles; the on-screen REWIND button spends an echo (3 → 2, one echo spawned,
  `loopIndex` +1). Touch targets are 74px (ACT) and 62px (REWIND).
* **Leaderboard chain** (verified 2026-08-14, unchanged since): `GET
  /api/leaderboard/rank?gameId=paradox-vault&score=N` then `POST
  /api/leaderboard/submit {gameId,name,score}`, matching the Cloudflare
  Functions' contracts exactly.
* Home-page integration asserted in-browser: featured heading, play href,
  `leaderboard.html?gameId=paradox-vault`, and the
  `.rate[data-id]` + `<span class="score">` pairing the rating system needs.

## Fixed tonight

**Lighting ambient floor** (`render.js`). The in-flight render rework replaced
"multiply by flat grey, then screen the lights back on" with a single multiply
against a light buffer whose base is the ambient term. The architecture is an
improvement, but multiply can never brighten, and with `AMBIENT` at
`rgb(31,38,55)` every surface the level did not directly light crushed to
near-black — floor texture, the deco border band and the player figure were all
effectively invisible at 1440x900. A side-by-side against the last
critic-reviewed build (`bdac1e9`) confirmed this as a legibility regression
rather than an art choice. Raised to `rgb(60,70,94)` / `rgb(80,90,116)`.

## Carried polish debt — an honest list

1. **No critic ever passed.** Every discipline above is unreviewed against a
   shipped AAA reference. The art, VFX, HUD and audio have never been judged.
2. **Audio has never been heard by anything.** No run has listened to it or
   analysed it offline. It is completely unverified.
3. **The 60fps target is unverified and cannot be verified in this sandbox.**
   There is no GPU here — Chromium reports SwiftShader under every launch
   configuration tried — so every fps number any run has recorded is
   software-rasterised and valid only as a relative before/after measure. This
   needs a human to open the game on real hardware. Treat as permanent carried
   debt, not a to-do.
4. **`PV.Textures.bakeMs` is 330–500 ms**, over SPEC §4's "< ~400ms on a phone"
   budget before a phone's slower CPU is accounted for.
5. **Portrait phones show a lot of dead space.** A 16x12 room in a 9:19.5
   viewport is inherently small. The room is no longer cropped (fixed
   2026-08-12), but portrait-aware framing or taller vault layouts would help.
6. **Only 5 of 10 vaults truly require the signature mechanic.** Vaults 4, 6 and
   7 have an echo cost of 0 — gated by hazards or a latching receiver rather
   than by holding a signal. Deliberate variety, but thin for a game whose whole
   identity is the time loop. The design suite asserts this ratio does not get
   worse.
7. **Nothing has been play-tested for difficulty or fun.** The suites prove each
   vault is solvable inside its echo budget; nobody has judged whether any vault
   is *interesting*.
8. The title-screen tagline "Rob the vault. Then help yourself do it." wraps to
   an orphaned "it." at 1440x900 — a `max-width` nitpick on the first screen a
   player sees.
9. The game is not offline-clean per SPEC §0: `index.html` carries the arcade's
   analytics tag like every other game page here. Deliberate.
10. `ui.js` contains a stray NUL byte used as an unset sentinel
    (`carrying: '\x00'`). Harmless and intentional, but surprising — `file(1)`
    reports the source as binary.
